  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 205 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSunday...Main challenge will be the heat as near perfect setup for \nnear record high temperatures, with the dominant 5960 meter 500 mb \nridge axis prevailing and centered over the Four Corners. With quite \ndry air at low and mid levels, and 700 mb temperatures near 20 to 23 \nCelsius, we expect maximum temperatures in the mid 90s to near 100 \ndegrees west of I-25, with upper 90s to near 108 degrees east of I-\n25, with the hottest temperatures up in Sioux, Dawes and Box \nButte Counties of the northern Nebraska Panhandle. \n\nCurrent record high temperatures for August 2nd include...\n\n98 degrees in 2008 at Cheyenne\n94 degrees in 2024 at Laramie \n98 degrees in 2000 at Rawlins\n106 degrees in 2008 at Alliance\n104 degrees in 2022 at Chadron\n104 degrees in 1938 at Scottsbluff \n103 degrees in 2008 at Sidney\n\nLooking at our projected high temperatures, it looks like near \nrecord high temperatures expected at Rawlins, Laramie, Cheyenne, \nChadron and Scottsbluff. Fortunately, dewpoints will be relatively \nlow and heat index temperatures will peak in the 90s to near 100 \ndegrees east of I-25. We will need to monitor minimum temperature \ntrends to see about the necessity for possible Heat Advisories for \nSunday in portions of the Nebraska Panhandle. \n\nOn Monday, the ridge flattens somewhat as a weak shortwave trough \naloft and its associated cold front enter our counties from the \nnorth, cooling off temperatures somewhat and there may be enough \navailable moisture for isolated late day thunderstorms over the \nsouthern Laramie Range. \n\nThen on Tuesday, expect a relief from the summertime heat in the \nwake of a passing Canadian origin cold front, sending maximum \ntemperatures back into the mid 70s to mid 80s. Moisture looks scant, \nperhaps enough for isolated to widely scattered late day \nthunderstorms near the mountains. \n\nFor Wednesday...synoptically, we anticipate a return flow event as \nlow level southeast winds advect more low level moisture \nnorthwestward into our counties and providing enough fuel to help \ndevelop scattered afternoon and evening thunderstorms primarily from \nI-25 eastward, and we will likely need to increase our POPS for this \nday in the next few days. \n\nOn Thursday, a return to the heat is on tap under northwest flow \naloft and with drier air infiltrating the counties, more heat is \nexpected as 700 mb temperatures rise to near 15 Celsius, yielding \nmaximum temperatures in the 90s to near 100 degrees.",
